From 78e7cf421c824db7dcfe71a0c53175651b20ead6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: facetimehd downstream <facetimehd-downstream@localhost>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 06/19] pci: symmetric bring-up and teardown, with runtime
 power management

Upstream's PM callbacks were fthd_pci_remove() and fthd_pci_probe(): a
system suspend tore the driver down completely, including the V4L2 device,
and a resume built a new one. Any file descriptor an application held across
a suspend pointed at freed memory afterwards.

Split the hardware transition out of probe/remove into a pair of functions
that only ever touch hardware:

  - fthd_pm_down() stops the firmware, releases the interrupt, the BARs and
    the DMA buffers, and hands the device back to the PCI core for D3.
  - fthd_pm_up() reverses it: powering the ISP off loses the DDR training
    and the firmware image, so both are redone from scratch.

dev_priv, the V4L2 registration and the debugfs tree survive across that, so
/dev/videoN and every open fd outlive a power cycle. Both are idempotent and
run under a new pm_lock; the runtime callbacks take only that lock, since a
runtime resume is reached from open() with no V4L2 lock held.

On top of that:

  - Real dev_pm_ops, with runtime PM selected by facetimehd.runtime_pm=1.
    An open video file holds a reference, the camera autosuspends five
    seconds after the last close, and the next open reloads its firmware.
    The module default leaves the device powered, so bare module discovery
    never schedules an ISP/DDR/PCI teardown.
  - dev_priv is refcounted. The video_device can outlive PCI unbind while an
    fd is open, so it takes a reference and drops it from its release
    callback; ->removing makes open() and the debugfs accessors fail with
    -ENODEV once removal starts.
  - The BARs are requested and released as a set, mapped with pci_iomap()
    and unmapped in one place, and a failed mapping unwinds instead of
    leaving three half-mapped BARs behind. isp_init() failing now runs
    isp_uninit(), and isp_free_channel_info() clears the channel pointers it
    frees rather than leaving them dangling.
  - vdev->lock and the vb2 queue lock become ioctl_lock, so the ioctl,
    control and debugfs paths serialise against each other, and
    fthd_v4l2_register() unwinds each stage it got through.
